WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.090
- I spend all day looking
at radicalization pathways

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.560
and the worst possible things
that can happen online.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.430
- In terms of tracking, my
biggest concern this time

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.710
isn't Russian interference
in the election,

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.670
it's American mobilizations
at polling places,

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.990
threats of violence in terms of counting

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.070
or ballots or ballot boxes.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.630
- Election security
officials and academics

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.760
say misinformation and disinformation

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.925
have never been more of a
threat to the U.S. election,

00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:29.690
especially as coronavirus concerns

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.440
breed more confusion
around the voting process.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.560
From misleading text messages
to false social media posts

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.290
shared by seemingly trusted sources,

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:41.000
bad actors are targeting many groups.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.450
And often, the researchers
tracking the content

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.420
find themselves scrambling to keep up

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.870
with those spreading the false
or misleading information.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.040
The researchers flag it to the platforms,

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.490
which also have their
own monitoring services.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.426
What's at stake for the
2020 presidential election?

00:00:56.426 --> 00:00:58.360
What's the worst that could happen

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:00.700
if misinformation runs rampant?

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.260
- What are we opening ourselves up to?

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.300
How are we normalizing the ingestion

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.670
of information in quick,
10-slide Instagram Stories?

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.660
- [Emily] Isabella Garcia-Camargo

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.100
is a cybersecurity researcher at Stanford

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.710
working on a coalition
focused on detecting

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:16.900
and mitigating misinformation

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.370
during the election.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:19.640
- Even a year ago, a lot of people,

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.310
if you talked about QAnon,
nobody really knew what it was.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.470
Since COVID, though, and
since the huge change

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.050
in kind of the information landscape

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.030
that came from that, the
movement has really grown

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.070
over the past couple of
months, and we're seeing it

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.910
pop up internationally even now.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.190
- [Emily] The FBI has identified QAnon

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.850
as a potential domestic terrorism threat.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.530
The far-right-wing,
loosely organized network

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.549
is one of the conspiracy theory groups

00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:45.530
that has gained traction
since social distancing

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:49.690
forced many people to more
frequently interact online.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.920
And it's not just online platforms.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.700
Misinformation is spreading
more through text messages

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.030
or digital television ads,
especially as social media firms

00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.892
crack down on such content.

00:01:59.892 --> 00:02:01.510
- [Isabella] I think it's
important to recognize

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.730
that more and more young
people are getting their news

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.800
from TikTok, getting
their news from Instagram.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.800
- People are isolated and they are trying

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.830
to figure out the world
from a digital feed.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.080
Well, if that digital
feed is being manipulated

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.890
or bombarded with false information,

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:19.070
or being distorted through
paid advertisements,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.020
it makes it very difficult for people

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:22.330
to understand what's going on.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.763
- [Emily] Clint Watts is
a former FBI special agent

00:02:24.763 --> 00:02:26.540
and a distinguished fellow

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.780
at the Foreign Policy Research Institute

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.000
studying social media influence.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.610
Election security experts like Clint

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.200
say inaccurate voter
or polling information

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.660
could mean some miss their chance to vote.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.320
While social media giants
like Facebook and Twitter

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.010
now have more stringent
policies against content

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.560
that could suppress
voters, security officials

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.390
are still worried about the damage done

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:51.550
before misinformation
is labeled or removed.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.830
Conspiracies that claim mail-in ballots

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.280
aren't counted could dissuade some voters,

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.020
and questions about the
validity of election results

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.700
could threaten the future
of politics in America.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:02.780
- [Isabella] It's less one candidate

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.780
will have an unfair advantage
or the other one will.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.580
It's more like just generally
mistrust in the outcome

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.280
from the losing side
or from any side here.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.750
If we start to erode
the trust that we have

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.900
in our democratic principles
and in our democratic system,

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.168
I think that that has very,
very long-term consequences.

00:03:21.168 --> 00:03:23.174
- [Clint] I want people to
know that their vote counted,

00:03:23.174 --> 00:03:24.900
and I want them to have
confidence in that.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.960
- [Emily] Misinformation
has already spread

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.420
in this election cycle.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.340
The legitimacy of mail-in ballots

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:32.880
has been questioned by
some, and in August,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.740
a number of Florida voters received

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.650
a spoofed text message
alleging Rep. Byron Donalds

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.850
was dropping out of a congressional race.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.210
Soon after, Donalds tweeted
that the text message

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:43.930
shared false information, and he,

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.540
in fact, was not dropping out.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.800
- If you continue to pump disinformation

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.120
through the social media machine

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.500
and social media doesn't
regulate, to a degree,

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:56.390
what is actual verifiable
truth, lets deepfakes continue,

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:59.190
those sorts of things, well,
everyone loses in the end.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.810
The companies fall into
a collapsing state,

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.580
the politicians can't advance policy,

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.600
and voters are just sorta
left hanging in the balance.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.420
- Every single time
that you post something

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.850
to your Story, that you like a tweet,

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.310
your interactions are
like a vote in this space.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.400
The most patriotic thing you can do

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.730
is to just think a little
bit before you like,

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:20.433
or you tweet, or you share things.

